11:28:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Good question—there are a few things going on here, and they're mostly about what "65 degrees" is actually measuring and controlling. **Humidity is a huge factor** AC does two things: cools air and removes moisture (dehumidifies). Air at 65°F with high humidity feels different—often clammier or heavier—than 65°F with low humidity. When AC is running, it's actively pulling moisture out of the air, so that 65° feels crisp and dry. If you're hitting 65° without AC (say, just outside air on a mild day), the humidity could be much higher, making it feel cooler/damper or just different on your skin even at the same thermometer reading. **Airflow and evaporative cooling** AC systems typically run the fan more aggressively to hit and maintain a temperature, and that moving air passes over your skin, causing evaporative cooling. This makes the air feel colder than still air at the same temperature. If your car reaches 65° with the AC off (windows down, cool outside temp, etc.), airflow patterns are totally different—less concentrated, less directed at you—so it feels different even if the number matches. **Surface temperatures** The thermometer only measures air temp, but your comfort is also affected by the temperature of surfaces around you—the seats, dashboard, windows. If it's warm outside and the AC is working hard to get the air to 65°, the dashboard and windows might still be radiating heat at you (think of sitting near a window with sun coming through). Without AC, if the whole car has equilibrated with cooler outside air, those surfaces are also cooler, so there's less radiant heat hitting your body. **The thermometer/sensor location** Car climate systems often measure temperature at one point (usually near the dash), not evenly throughout the cabin. So "65°" might be accurate right at the sensor but not representative of the temperature where you're sitting. **Bottom line:** temperature is just one piece of how "cold" or "warm" something feels. Humidity, air movement, and radiant heat from surfaces all shape your actual comfort—so identical thermometer readings can feel quite different depending on those other factors.
