11:29:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, this is a great example to bring up—because ovens prove my point rather than contradicting it. **Convection ovens cook faster and differently than regular ovens at the same temperature.** That's exactly why convection recipes tell you to either lower the temperature (usually by 25°F) or shorten the cook time compared to a standard oven. A 350° convection oven browns food faster and cooks more evenly than a 350° non-convection oven, even though the air temperature is identical in both cases. Why? Same reason as the car: moving air transfers heat to (or from) a surface faster than still air does. In a regular oven, a layer of slightly cooler air tends to cling to the food's surface, insulating it somewhat and slowing heat transfer. A fan constantly sweeps that boundary layer away, so hot air is continuously in direct contact with the food, transferring heat more efficiently. The oven's air is 350° in both cases—but the *rate* at which that heat gets into the food is different. This is the exact same physics as AC blowing on your skin. The air temperature reads 65° either way, but moving air disrupts the thin layer of insulating air around your skin, so heat leaves your body faster, and it feels cooler. So actually, if you polled experienced bakers, they'd back me up: 350 is not 350 in practice, when one has a fan and one doesn't. That's a well-known, practical cooking fact, not a controversial one. The oven analogy isn't proof that identical temperatures behave identically—it's proof of the opposite.
